    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The presence of multiple integrals in the common-family effect and polygenic likelihood functions usually means that considerable computing power is required to obtain the MLEs in even modest pedigrees. Knott et al. (1990, 1991a) provide excellent approximations for these Gaussian integrals, decreasing computational requirements. However, since multiple local maxima can occur on the likelihood surface, care must still be taken in numerically computing the global likelihood (Demenais et al. 1986, Borecki et al. 1995).",
